Barometric Palm
Ingredients: HIGHLIFE + AMBIENT + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Brass
Creative Catalyst: The genre honors a lost pirate radio station along the Ghanaian coast that only broadcast during torrential thunderstorms when the municipal power grid failed.

Barometric Palm fuses the fleet-fingered, major-key optimism of coastal West African highlife with expansive, tape-degraded ambient drone textures and jubilant, full-throated brass fanfares. Propelled by galloping bell polyrhythms and sparkling twin-guitar counterpoint, the music floats inside humid, cloud-bursting acoustic spaces where deep thunder-rumble sub-bass grounds ecstatic horn melodies.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 112–124 BPM; a rolling, buoyant 6/8 cross-rhythm layered over a steady, swung 4/4 highlife pulse driven by iron gankogui bells, warm wooden congas, and breezy maraca syncopations.
- Harmonic Palette: Exclusively sun-drenched diatonic major modes (predominantly F major and B-flat major) using cascading I–IV–I–V chord loops, suspended over lush, unresolved synth drones (add9 and sus2 voicings) that evoke heavy, pre-storm humidity.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Squall-swell brass: Live horns captured with ribbon microphones in stone stairwells and treated with 100% wet analog plate reverbs that bloom and linger like rolling mist.
  2. Low-frequency storm beds: Sub-audible field recordings of real sea squalls and rumbling barometric pressure drops layered beneath the electric bassline to anchor the low end.
  3. Reel-to-reel tape flutter: Clean, interlocking Stratocaster arpeggios run through overdriven tape decks with fluttering capstans, mimicking the sound of distant coastal broadcasts slipping in and out of phase.
- Dynamics: Songs rise out of quiet, oceanic ambient drones and crackling radio hiss before abruptly detonating into high-energy, brass-fueled celebrations; arrangements continually alternate between suspended "eye-of-the-storm" instrumental ambient washes and full-ensemble, joyous dance eruptions.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound traces back to the late 1970s in the twin harbor cities of Sekondi-Takoradi, Ghana. Whenever monsoon squalls knocked out the state power grid and plunged the coast into darkness, a clandestine broadcast known as "Radio Barometric" fired up on maritime car batteries, piping euphoric highlife brass and shimmering tape loops through the rain. In response, dockworkers, off-duty brass band players, and cassette bootleggers gathered in zinc-roofed verandahs wearing bright wax-print rain ponchos and heavy rubber sea-boots. They brought valve trombones, palm-wine guitars, and battery-powered portable organs, jamming directly over the storm's natural percussion while drinking chilled palm wine. The gatherings became legendary "squall-dances"—transcendent, joyful midnight parties that lasted only as long as the rainfall, vanishing the moment the municipal electricity returned.

Typical Instruments: 
- Central Brass: Flugelhorn, B-flat trumpet, valve trombone, and tenor saxophone
- Guitars & Percussion: Dual clean electric guitars (Stratocaster style), gankogui (iron double-bell), open-tuned wooden congas, apentemma hand drums, and warm electric P-bass
- Synthesizers & Atmosphere: Roland Juno-60 ambient pads, Revox A77 tape loop delay systems, hydrophone microphones, and battery-operated field cassette recorders

For Fans Of: Ebo Taylor, Gigi Masin, Osibisa, Jon Hassell

