Track 3: The Zinc Meridian Dances
- Story & Context: Long after the clandestine transmitter vanished into coastal folklore, villagers along the western beaches insisted that a decommissioned metal beacon outside Half Assini continued to resonate with four-part horn harmonies during August downpours. In 1989, local net-maker Kwame Tawiah salvaged two wax-sealed reel-to-reel tapes hidden inside the hollow base of the tower, still smelling faintly of brine, machine grease, and wild frangipani blossoms. Hooking the salvaged tapes to a portable boombox in the village square as a squall rolled in, the ecstatic trumpet choruses uncannily fell into step with the timing of the ocean breakers, sending the entire town dancing through the ankle-deep runoff. Over shimmering, tape-saturated ambient guitar delays, the recording preserved the station's final collective chant: "No map can hold the horn when the rain begins to stomp!" Today, whenever thunder shakes the municipal power grid offline, the town's youth return to the zinc tower to dance in the joyous mud.
